1. Create an instance of the AEAD GCM cipher class.
2. Initialize the cipher with the appropriate key and nonce.
3. Call the `updateAAD` method with the additional authenticated data (AAD) you want to include in the encryption or decryption process.
4. Continue using the cipher object for encryption or decryption as needed.

Here's some example code demonstrating how to use the `updateAAD` method:
```java
import java.security.Key;
import java.security.SecretKeySpec;
import java.util.Arrays;

import javax.crypto.Cipher;
import javax.crypto.spec.IvParameterSpec;

public class AeadGcmExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Generate a secret key and initialization vector (IV)
        Key secretKey = new SecretKeySpec("your_secret_key".getBytes(), "AES");
        IvParameterSpec ivParameterSpec = new IvParameterSpec("your_iv".getBytes());

        // Create an instance of the AEAD GCM cipher class
        Cipher cipher = Cipher.getInstance("AES/GCM/NoPadding");

        // Initialize the cipher with the secret key and IV
        cipher.init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, secretKey, ivParameterSpec);

        // Add some additional authenticated data (AAD)
        byte[] aad = "your_aad".getBytes();
        cipher.updateAAD(aad);

        // Encrypt some data using the cipher
        byte[] plaintext = "your_plaintext".getBytes();
        byte[] encryptedData = cipher.doFinal(plaintext);

        // Decrypt the encrypted data using the cipher
        byte[] decryptedData = cipher.doFinal(encryptedData);

        // Check that the decrypted data matches the original plaintext
        assert Arrays.equals(plaintext, decryptedData);
    }
}
```
