To divide an hour into 10 equal parts, you can use the following formula in Excel:

`=TIME(HOUR($A2), MINUTE($A2)/6, SECOND($A2))`

Assuming your data is in cell A2, this formula will return the first 10-minute interval of the hour. You can then copy and paste this formula down for the remaining intervals.

In MATLAB, you can use the following code to achieve the same result:

```matlab
% Assuming your data is in column 'A'
hour = A(:,1);
minute = A(:,2);
second = A(:,3);

% Calculate the number of minutes per hour
minutes_per_hour = 60;

% Calculate the start time of the first 10-minute interval
start_time = datetime(hour, minute(1:10:minutes_per_hour), second(1:10:minutes_per_hour));

% Create a vector of start times for all 10-minute intervals
start_times = start_time + minutes_per_hour*(0:9);

% Calculate the end time of each 10-minute interval
end_times = start_times + minutes_per_hour;
```

This code will calculate the start and end times of each 10-minute interval for the entire hour. You can then use these times to perform more granular analysis on your data.

Here's a Go program that parses a struct in a Go source file and generates an Avro schema from it. The program takes the name of the Go source file and the name of the struct as input, and outputs a file named `struct.avsc`.

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"io/ioutil"
	"reflect"
	"strings"

	"github.com/apache/avro/v2/schema"
)

func main() {
	file := "example.go"
	structName := "MyStruct"

	code, err := ioutil.ReadFile(file)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Println("Error reading file:", err)
		return
	}

	tokens := strings.Split(string(code), "\n")

	for _, token := range tokens {
		if strings.HasPrefix(token, "type") {
			parts := strings.SplitN(token, " ", 2)
			if len(parts) == 2 {
				structType := parts[1]
				if structType == structName {
					fmt.Println("Found struct definition:")
					fmt.Println(token)

					fields := reflect.StructOf(reflect.TypeOf(structName).Elem())
					avroSchema := schema.MakeRecordSchema(structName, fields)

					err = ioutil.WriteFile("struct.avsc", []byte(avroSchema.ToString()), 0644)
					if err != nil {
						fmt.Println("Error writing file:", err)
						return
					}

					fmt.Println("Avro schema generated successfully!")
					return
				}
			}
		}
	}

	fmt.Println("Struct not found in file.")
}
```

