Here's a simple decorator for Nest.js middleware that checks the NODE_ENV variable and returns `true` or `false` accordingly:

```typescript
import { Injectable, CanActivate, ExecutionContext } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class EnvGuard implements CanActivate {
  canActivate(context: ExecutionContext): boolean {
    const request = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();
    const nodeEnv = request.env.NODE_ENV;

    if (nodeEnv === 'production') {
      return false;
    } else {
      return true;
    }
  }
}
```

To use this guard, you can add it to your controller's methods like this:

```typescript
import { Controller, Get } from '@nestjs/common';
import { EnvGuard } from './env.guard';

@Controller('your-controller-path')
export class YourController {
  constructor(private readonly envGuard: EnvGuard) {}

  @Get()
  async getData(): Promise<any> {
    await this.envGuard.canActivate(this);
    // Your logic here
  }
}
```

This way, the guard will be executed before the `getData` method, and if the NODE_ENV is set to 'production', the method will not be called, and the response will be false. Otherwise, the method will be called, and the response will be true.

