To inject JavaScript into an existing webpage using your Chrome extension, you can use the `chrome.tabs` API. Here's a step-by-step guide to help you configure the boilerplate files:

1. Create a manifest file (manifest.json):

```json
{
  "manifest_version": 2,
  "name": "Your Extension Name",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "A Chrome extension that injects JavaScript into webpages.",
  "browser_action": {
    "default_icon": "icon.png",
    "default_popup": "popup.html"
  },
  "permissions": ["activeTab", "tabs"]
}
```

Replace `Your Extension Name`, `icon.png`, and `popup.html` with appropriate values for your extension.

2. Create a popup HTML file (popup.html):

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <script src="popup.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
  <button id="inject">Inject JavaScript</button>
</body>
</html>
```

3. Create a popup JavaScript file (popup.js):

```javascript
document.getElementById("inject").addEventListener("click", () => {
  chrome.tabs.query({ active: true, currentWindow: true }, tabs => {
    chrome.tabs.executeScript(tabs[0].id, { file: "content.js" });
  });
});
```

4. Create a content script file (content.js):

```javascript
document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", () => {
  const script = document.createElement("script");
  script.src = "your_javascript_file.js";
  document.body.appendChild(script);
});
```

Replace `your_javascript_file.js` with the path to your JavaScript file that you want to inject into the webpage.

5. Create an icon image file (icon.png):

This is optional, but if you want to add an icon to your extension, create an icon image file (e.g., icon.png) with the desired

